{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And that which remains, etc.</b> One needs to know why the verse repeated this twice. And perhaps it may be explained on the basis of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Menahot 56a) concerning all meal-offerings, that it is permitted to eat the remnant of meal-offerings with honey, but if one leavened their remnant one incurs lashes — and they derived this (Menahot 55a) from what the verse says: ״לֹא תֵאָפֶה חָמֵץ חֶלְקָם״ — meaning, also their portion, end. And yet I would say that he incurs lashes only for the baking, but if he leavened it and did not bake it, not so. Therefore the verse says: ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת וְגוֹ׳ כָּל הַמִּנְחָה אֲשֶׁר תַּקְרִיבוּ לֹא תֵעָשֶׂה חָמֵץ״ — meaning, even its remnant. And although our Sages of blessed memory (ibid.) derived it from the verse ״לֹא תֵאָפֶה חָמֵץ״ which is followed by ״חֶלְקָם״, from that derashah one would not incur liability for its remnant except for baking and not for any other action, whereas from this verse alone it would not suffice to incur liability for its remnant in every single detail. And although they derived there in Torat Kohanim that baking was in the general category and why did it come out separately — to tell you: just as baking is an individual act and one is liable for it, so I will bring every individual act — yet I would say that this refers only to its handful and not to its remnant; hence Scripture teaches us ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת״ וְגוֹ׳." "It further may be explained on the basis of what is said in Torat Kohanim: ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת״ — even though it was not salted, even though it was not presented (hugshah), even though he did not burn all of its frankincense; ״מִן הַמִּנְחָה״ — to exclude one that was lacking, etc., and one who did not burn any of its frankincense at all. And the Raavad wrote that the vav and the heh are a ribbuy (inclusory particle) to include what was mentioned up to this point. But my mind does not grasp the extent of this ribbuy, for the word carries no [changed] meaning if one removes the vav and the heh. And Ba'al Korban Aharon explained that the vav alone is what is additional, and even regarding this one may raise a question. And furthermore, from where does the Tanna derive three ribbuyim: salting, presentation, and all the frankincense? Granted, what it excludes with [the phrase] ״from the meal-offering״ — one may derive every deficiency that exists in a meal-offering as disqualifying; but a single ribbuy does not give us license to include many particulars." "<b>However,</b> it appears that since the verse stated these three commandments — salting, presentation, and the burning of all its frankincense — scattered in three places: the burning of all its frankincense it wrote in the section of the meal-offering of fine flour; the presentation in the meal-offering of the pan; the salting after the completion of all the laws of meal-offerings. And we find that the verse said ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת מִן הַמִּנְחָה״ after the mention of the meal-offering of fine flour, even though presentation was not mentioned therein. From this you learn that presentation does not disqualify it, for behold, even though presentation was not mentioned therein, it said ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת וְגוֹ׳ לְאַהֲרֹן וּבָנָיו״. And although we say based on the derashah of juxtaposition and the vav that is stated at the beginning of each section and section — that the verse intends to apply what is stated of this one to that one, and if so the presentation that was stated regarding the meal-offering of the pan must also apply to the meal-offering of fine flour, etc. — from the fact that it wrote ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת״ in the middle of the meal-offerings, it revealed that the inference is only for the initial requirement but it does not disqualify on its account; but all the frankincense is disqualifying. And furthermore, we find that the verse turned back and said ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת״ וְגוֹ׳ after the law of the meal-offering of the pan, and this verse — everyone who sees it will declare that it is itself redundant, for these matters were stated as written above. This surely came only to say that regarding the specific laws recorded there, it suffices for there to be ״that which remains״ for Aaron etc., and there the burning of frankincense was not mentioned at all but only the handful, as it is written ״וְהֵרִים הַכֹּהֵן וְגוֹ׳ אַזְכָּרָתָהּ״ — meaning the handful — and frankincense was not mentioned. From this you learn that it was for this that the verse came to teach: even though he did not burn all the frankincense as recorded in the section of the meal-offering of fine flour, ״that which remains״ is for Aaron. And this is the statement of the Tanna: ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת״ — even though it was not presented, even though he did not burn all its frankincense. And it also taught: even though it was not salted, for we saw that He commanded after His saying ״וְהַנּוֹתֶרֶת לְאַהֲרֹן״ — from this you learn that the remnant is permitted even though it was not salted."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:01.241545686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}